#! The byte-stability claim. `regions.lu` is canonical `wolf fmt` output at
#! the pin, so formatting it must produce ZERO edits — not an edit that
#! happens to be a no-op, an empty array. An editor that applies a whole-file
#! replacement on every save churns the undo stack and defeats every
#! format-on-save workflow, and the result is `exact` here for that reason.
#!
#! THE ONE TRANSCRIPT THAT MOVED AT THE v0.2.16 PIN, AND IT MOVED FOR THE
#! DOCUMENT, NOT FOR THE SERVER. tl11 re-recorded all 71 scripted sessions at
#! `93a5fe5`: 71 files changed, 72 insertions, 72 deletions — seventy are
#! header-only (`wolf_pin`, `recorded`), and this one is the seventy-first,
#! with exactly TWO changed lines: its header, and line 5. Line 5 is a `c2s`
#! `textDocument/didOpen`, which is the CLIENT's own payload: the vendored
#! `regions.lu` changed one line of its `//!` header directive between the
#! tags —
#!
#!     -//! phase: mem
#!     +//! phase: run
#!
#! — wolf-lang `ac256b20` (s170, "Pool[T] and handle T lower — three refusals
#! narrowed, three witnesses mem -> run"). The program below that header is
#! byte-for-byte what it was, and the FORMATTING RESPONSE did not move at all:
#! it is still the empty edit array this transcript exists to pin. Across all
#! 71 transcripts, **not one `s2c` line changed** — the server's answer to
#! every request in the library is byte-identical at 0.2.15 and 0.2.16.
#!
#! Kept here because the two causes read the same in a diff and are not the
#! same claim. tl11 predicted zero movers, having reasoned only about what the
#! COMPILER changed, and never checked whether the vendored INPUTS had moved —
#! which tl09 measured explicitly and this lane assumed. The prediction was
#! wrong by one, and this is the one.
name      requests/formatting-byte-stable
profile   minimal

initialize
open      regions.lu
wait      diagnostics regions.lu
req       formatting regions.lu
shutdown
